  "answer_text": "Here is a comprehensive breakdown of the word **INDOLENT**:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcd6 Definition\n\n**Indolent** is an adjective meaning **slow or lazy**. More specifically, it means **averse to activity, effort, or movement; habitually lazy.**\n\nAn indolent person is slow and lazy \u2014 not the type of person you'd want running your corporation or competing with you in a relay race.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udfe5 Medical Usage\n\nThe word also has a specialized meaning in medicine. Doctors use the word *indolent* to describe medical conditions that are **slow to progress**. If you're diagnosed with an illness, you'd prefer an indolent one over one that spreads quickly.\n\nAdditionally, in pathology, *indolent* can mean **causing little pain**, or (especially of a painless ulcer) **slow to heal**.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf31 Etymology (Word Origin)\n\nThe word comes from **Latin *indol\u0113ns***, meaning \"not feeling pain,\" from *in-* (not) + *dol\u0113ns*, from *dol\u0113re* meaning \"to grieve\" or \"cause distress.\"\n\nThe modern sense of \"laziness, love of ease\" dates to around **1710**, perhaps reflecting the notion of avoiding trouble.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd24 Word Forms\n- **Adjective:** indolent *(e.g., \"an indolent worker\")*\n- **Noun:** indolence *(e.g., \"a life of indolence\")*\n- **Adverb:** indolently\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd01 Synonyms\nSynonyms of *indolent* include: **lazy, slack, idle, slow, sluggish, inactive, inert, languid, lethargic, listless, lackadaisical, torpid, slothful.**\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcdd Example Sentence\n*\"The indolent student rarely completed his homework on time and spent most of the day lounging around.\"*",
